# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-will-antecedent.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3 and aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3.3 have "wills → nec_by_cause"; aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "God wills a thing" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-will-antecedent.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3 and aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "God wills a thing" is true and "the thing is necessary by its antecedent cause, the divine will" is false. Is S3, "Now things created by God are related to the divine will as to an antecedent cause, whereby they have necessity.", true then?
aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3 and aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3.3 have nothing from it; aquinas-will-antecedent.logic.r3.2 has "□(wills → comes) → (wills → nec_by_cause)"; they differ when only "God wills a thing" and "necessarily, (if God wills a thing, then the thing God wills comes to pass)" are true, which the first allows
